WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.912
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:06.805
a HashSet to remember unique String values,

00:00:06.805 --> 00:00:09.967
check membership, and interpret boolean results

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:12.994
that say whether an addition changed the set.

00:00:13.654 --> 00:00:16.935
These operations help applications recognize

00:00:16.935 --> 00:00:20.216
repeated categories and build distinct-value

00:00:20.216 --> 00:00:23.795
reports, so another request for an existing kind

00:00:23.795 --> 00:00:26.629
isn't mistaken for a new kind of data.

00:00:27.321 --> 00:00:30.711
A campus supply desk receives requests for pins

00:00:30.711 --> 00:00:31.360
and tags.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.679
Each name represents a kind of supply, not the

00:00:34.679 --> 00:00:37.203
number of physical items requested.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.593
The desk needs to recognize a repeated name and

00:00:40.593 --> 00:00:43.046
check which kinds it has recorded.

00:00:43.698 --> 00:00:46.475
We'll report whether each addition changes

00:00:46.475 --> 00:00:49.385
membership, compare a query before and after

00:00:49.385 --> 00:00:52.493
adding an item, and display the distinct count.

00:00:52.493 --> 00:00:55.138
After that worked case, you'll predict a

00:00:55.138 --> 00:00:58.048
different request sequence before we run it.

00:00:58.116 --> 00:01:01.359
Let's open Main.java and build the membership

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:01.791
check.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.378
Main is the class that contains our program, and

00:01:09.378 --> 00:01:12.220
its main method is where execution starts.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.333
We'll place the statements inside that method.

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:17.972
The import makes the HashSet class name

00:01:17.972 --> 00:01:20.881
available for the collection we're about to

00:01:20.881 --> 00:01:21.355
create.

00:01:29.838 --> 00:01:31.962
The program structure is ready.

00:01:31.962 --> 00:01:34.840
Now we'll create an empty set named items.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.061
String is the element type on both sides of the

00:01:38.061 --> 00:01:41.281
declaration, just as it was for an ArrayList of

00:01:41.281 --> 00:01:41.624
text.

00:01:41.624 --> 00:01:44.639
The new keyword creates this collection; the

00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:46.763
import alone doesn't create it.

00:01:52.605 --> 00:01:55.457
The declaration gives the program an empty

00:01:55.457 --> 00:01:56.816
starting collection.

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:59.804
A set keeps one representative of each equal

00:01:59.804 --> 00:02:02.452
value, and String equality compares the

00:02:02.452 --> 00:02:03.063
contents.

00:02:03.063 --> 00:02:06.187
Let's add a pin request and print the method's

00:02:06.187 --> 00:02:09.040
result so we can see whether that addition

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:10.330
changed membership.

00:02:17.511 --> 00:02:20.560
The first print will report true because pin

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.530
starts absent.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.649
The add call stores the new member before its

00:02:24.649 --> 00:02:27.421
returned boolean is joined to the label.

00:02:27.421 --> 00:02:30.470
Next we'll try the same text again, then ask

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.311
whether tag is present without adding it.

00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:52.596
The repeated pin call will return false because

00:02:52.596 --> 00:02:55.412
an equal value is already present; it won't

00:02:55.412 --> 00:02:57.181
create a second pin member.

00:02:57.181 --> 00:02:59.932
Contains is a different question: it reads

00:02:59.932 --> 00:03:03.010
whether a value is present and doesn't remember

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:03.796
a new value.

00:03:03.796 --> 00:03:06.678
Since tag hasn't been added, that query will

00:03:06.678 --> 00:03:07.792
return false too.

00:03:08.328 --> 00:03:11.186
Those two false results describe different

00:03:11.186 --> 00:03:11.935
operations.

00:03:11.935 --> 00:03:15.133
One says the duplicate addition made no change;

00:03:15.133 --> 00:03:17.106
the other says tag is absent.

00:03:17.106 --> 00:03:20.373
We'll now add tag, query it again, and print the

00:03:20.373 --> 00:03:23.503
number of distinct members after the requests.

00:03:43.778 --> 00:03:46.807
This standalone add changes membership without

00:03:46.807 --> 00:03:48.849
displaying its returned result.

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:52.009
The following query will find tag, and size will

00:03:52.009 --> 00:03:53.787
count the distinct members.

00:03:53.787 --> 00:03:56.882
We are asking about values, not list indices or

00:03:56.882 --> 00:03:58.660
a promised traversal order.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.624
Let's click Run and check the complete worked

00:04:01.624 --> 00:04:01.953
case.

00:04:07.453 --> 00:04:10.760
The first pin line is true and the repeated pin

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:11.745
line is false.

00:04:11.745 --> 00:04:14.982
Tag before add is false, then Tag after add is

00:04:14.982 --> 00:04:15.334
true.

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:17.585
The last line reports Unique: 2.

00:04:17.585 --> 00:04:20.752
Three addition attempts produced two distinct

00:04:20.752 --> 00:04:24.129
members, and the contains queries did not change

00:04:24.129 --> 00:04:24.903
that count.

00:04:25.612 --> 00:04:28.757
A false add result is normal duplicate handling,

00:04:28.757 --> 00:04:29.871
not a Java error.

00:04:29.871 --> 00:04:32.885
The final count describes the different String

00:04:32.885 --> 00:04:35.571
values present, rather than the number of

00:04:35.571 --> 00:04:38.126
requests or the number of method calls.

00:04:38.126 --> 00:04:41.206
With that distinction established, we can apply

00:04:41.206 --> 00:04:43.237
the same rules to a new report.

00:04:43.286 --> 00:04:46.447
Let's replace the worked program with the supply

00:04:46.447 --> 00:04:48.159
desk's map and kit report.

00:04:48.159 --> 00:04:51.122
We'll start a fresh empty set, then print the

00:04:51.122 --> 00:04:54.282
results of a first map request and another equal

00:04:54.282 --> 00:04:55.073
map request.

00:04:55.073 --> 00:04:58.233
Keep your predicted results to yourself while we

00:04:58.233 --> 00:05:00.011
enter the complete program.

00:05:27.687 --> 00:05:30.867
The new setup and two labeled map attempts are

00:05:30.867 --> 00:05:31.490
in place.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.393
We'll add kit without printing that call's

00:05:34.393 --> 00:05:37.366
result, then ask whether kit is present and

00:05:37.366 --> 00:05:39.025
report the unique count.

00:05:39.025 --> 00:05:42.068
The final queries read the state left by the

00:05:42.068 --> 00:05:43.312
earlier additions.

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:02.777
The new program is complete.

00:06:02.777 --> 00:06:05.751
Before clicking Run, predict all four labeled

00:06:05.751 --> 00:06:06.610
output lines.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.716
Track whether each addition changes membership,

00:06:09.716 --> 00:06:12.425
and distinguish the query from the count.

00:06:12.425 --> 00:06:15.200
Pause here if you'd like more time to work

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:15.927
through it.

00:06:24.007 --> 00:06:27.058
Let's run this version and compare its output

00:06:27.058 --> 00:06:28.482
with your prediction.

00:06:34.051 --> 00:06:36.994
First map is true because no equal map was

00:06:36.994 --> 00:06:37.555
present.

00:06:37.555 --> 00:06:40.708
Duplicate map is false because the first call

00:06:40.708 --> 00:06:41.970
already stored it.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:45.264
Adding kit supplies another distinct member, so

00:06:45.264 --> 00:06:47.997
Contains kit is true and Unique is two.

00:06:47.997 --> 00:06:51.010
The query reads membership without adding a

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:51.851
third value.

00:06:52.613 --> 00:06:55.429
The desk's report now distinguishes new and

00:06:55.429 --> 00:06:58.573
repeated names, checks the resulting membership,

00:06:58.573 --> 00:07:00.276
and counts distinct kinds.

00:07:00.276 --> 00:07:03.157
HashSet provides that membership rule, while

00:07:03.157 --> 00:07:05.712
each returned boolean tells us what its

00:07:05.712 --> 00:07:07.349
particular operation did.

00:07:07.349 --> 00:07:10.427
We didn't need to assume any order for visiting

00:07:10.427 --> 00:07:11.213
set members.

00:07:11.949 --> 00:07:14.996
Suppose the desk asks whether pass is present

00:07:14.996 --> 00:07:16.215
but never adds it.

00:07:16.215 --> 00:07:19.194
What would that membership query report, and

00:07:19.194 --> 00:07:21.428
would it change the unique count?

00:07:21.428 --> 00:07:24.272
Explain which operation would be needed to

00:07:24.272 --> 00:07:26.574
remember pass for a later request.
